\section{Introduction}
This work presents an analysis of literary text, focusing on measuring sentiment and identifying named entities through natural language processing techniques. Sentiment is evaluated using three complementary methods: VADER for context-sensitive sentence-level scoring, a WordNet-based lexicon approach for sense-aware word-level sentiment, and TextBlob for sentence-level polarity analysis. The average sentiment for each method is calculated over both text chapters and textual blocks of defined length, independent of chapters, and the results are visualized.

In addition, the code identifies and tracks named entities throughout the text, recording their positions and categorizing them by type. This structured representation enables the mapping of characters, locations, times, and other entities, with the results also visualized.

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

This work relies on several natural language processing resources to perform sentiment and entity analysis on literary texts. 
For sentiment analysis, we use lexicon-based and rule-based approaches, complemented by Python libraries. 
For entity analysis, we employ NLP models capable of recognizing named entities and their positions in the text.

