\section{Approach}
The book \textit{Metamorphosis} by Franz Kafka was chosen for its intense emotional content. The entire code was written in Python, as it is a great language for NLP tasks, thanks to its rich ecosystem of libraries and tools specifically designed for text processing and analysis.

\subsection*{Sentiment Analysis}

The sentiment analysis begins by loading the text from a file using the \texttt{read\_document} function, which reads the full content of the text file and returns it as a string for further processing. The text is then cleaned using the \texttt{trim\_text\_edges} function, which removes a fixed number of lines from the beginning and end of the document to exclude non-narrative content.

Since chapters can vary significantly in size, with some being much larger than others, which can skew the analysis results, the text is divided into nearly equal-sized blocks, regardless of chapter boundaries, to mitigate this. The cleaned text is cut into segments of 7,000 characters, as this amount has been identified as an ideal block—neither too large nor too small—for our visualization. This is done using the \texttt{safe\_block\_split} function, which splits the text into blocks of a predefined maximum length while preserving sentence boundaries, ensuring that no sentence is split between blocks. As a result, most blocks are slightly shorter than the maximum size. The block locations are then calculated using the \texttt{cumulative\_text\_lengths} function, which accumulates the lengths of the blocks in words.

Similarly, the text is segmented into chapters using the \texttt{split\_and\_locate\_chapters} function, which uses a regular expression to identify chapter boundaries and returns separate chapter segments and their boundary locations.

For both chapters and text blocks, sentiment is computed using three methods implemented by the functions \texttt{vader\_senti\_sentences}, \texttt{spacytextblob\_senti\_sentences}, and \texttt{new\_wn\_senti\_sentences}. The WordNet-based method does not consider sentence context like the other two methods; instead, it calculates the average sentiment of all words in a sentence and considers this value the sentence sentiment. In all methods, sentiment is computed for individual sentences, which are treated as atomic units, and then averaged across the corresponding segments (blocks or chapters) to obtain a sentiment score per segment.

Finally, the program aggregates all computed sentiment values into a structured dictionary that associates segment positions (blocks and chapters) with their sentiment scores from all three methods. The results are printed and saved in JSON format using the \texttt{save\_json} function for further processing or visualization. The results are then visualized to illustrate sentiment progression using the \texttt{graph\_block\_and\_chapters\_sentiment} function.

