\section{Discussion}
\subsection*{Sentiment Visualization}
We can observe that the sentiment remains generally neutral, with higher sentiment peaks occurring in five distinct waves. Furthermore, the methods appear to share similar trends, which supports their apparent effectiveness. Overall, we can conclude that the sentiment is slightly positive, which is surprising given the book's typically depressive reputation.

\subsection*{Entities Visualization}
The data clearly show that Gregor is the main character, as he appears most frequently and consistently throughout the text. Interestingly, different references to the same person (e.g., \textit{Mrs. Samsa – Mother} ) don’t overlap, which seems to suggest a change in the narrator’s relationship with the characters, offering insights into the evolving dynamics between them.

\section{Conclusions}
In conclusion, this analysis applied natural language processing techniques to assess sentiment and identify named entities within the text. We measured sentiment across both chapters and defined blocks, with results visualized for clearer insights. Named entity recognition mapped the book’s characters and locations, which may shed light on character relationships and narrative dynamics. Overall, this work highlights the potential of NLP in deepening literary analysis.

